<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antonio Cánovas del Castillo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1828">1828</a>–<a href="/years/1897">1897</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antonio Cánovas del Castillo was a Spanish conservative statesman and historian who designed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> Restoration of 1874 and its Constitution of 1876. He led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservative-party-uk">Conservative Party</a> and created the turno pacífico, under which Conservatives and Liberals alternated in power. Prime minister six times, he was assassinated by an Italian anarchist in 1897.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonio-canovas-del-castillo.jpg" alt="Antonio Cánovas del Castillo" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fernando Debás · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CFP_Canovas_del_Castillo_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Antonio Cánovas del Castillo was born in Málaga and moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> after his father's death, living with his mother's cousin, the writer Serafín Estébanez Calderón. He studied law at the University of Madrid but showed an early interest in politics and Spanish history. His active political career began with the revolution of 1854 led by General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leopoldo-odonnell">Leopoldo O'Donnell</a>, when he drafted the Manifesto of Manzanares. In the last years of Isabel II he held the interior portfolio in 1864 and the overseas territories portfolio in 1865–66. During the First Spanish Republic he supported a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> restoration and drafted the Manifesto of Sandhurst for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xii">Alfonso XII</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After General Martínez Campos overthrew the Republic in 1874, Cánovas served as prime minister for six years, though he was twice briefly replaced, in 1875 and 1879. He was a principal author of the Constitution of 1876, which formalised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> and limited suffrage. He helped end the last Carlist threat in 1876 by merging dissident Carlist deputies with his Conservative party. His term also saw the decree of July 1876 that ended the Basque provinces' specific status.</p><p>After 1881 he alternated in office with the progressive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/praxedes-mateo-sagasta">Práxedes Mateo Sagasta</a> in the turno pacífico, an arrangement designed to reconcile the competing militarist, Catholic and Carlist power bases. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xii">Alfonso XII</a> died in 1885 he assumed the functions of head of state during the regency of María Cristina. He was also a historian, elected to the Real Academia de la Historia at 32 for his History of the Decline of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His repression of Cuban nationalists failed to prevent the rebellion of 1895 led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-marti">José Martí</a>, and conflict over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a> of 1898. After a bomb was thrown at a procession in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> in 1896, three hundred people were arrested and held at Montjuïc; reputedly Cánovas ordered their torture.</p><p>Cánovas was shot dead in 1897 at the spa of Santa Águeda in Mondragón by the Italian anarchist Michele Angiolillo, who invoked vengeance for the execution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-rizal">José Rizal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> anarchists. His peers nicknamed him el Monstruo. His restoration system gave way after the war to long political instability, which ended in the collapse of the monarchy and the constitution he had written.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antonio Cánovas del Castillo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The conservative statesman <b>Antonio Cánovas del Castillo</b> designed the new order and its Constitution of 1876.</blockquote><a href="/state/restoration-spain">Restoration Spain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Málaga, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Arrasate/Mondragón, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.72,-4.42&amp;year=1872" aria-label="Málaga on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4439 840 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Antonio Cánovas del Castillo"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4889 1110h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4889 1110h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.72,-4.42&amp;year=1872">What was here in 1872? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
